{"benchmark": "livebench_selected", "task_id": "paraphrase:f3472a74bc480a915c2a3875c505fd5b3ebe475d25cefdae9447002ed88b0106", "sample_idx": 41, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3-next-80b-a3b-instruct", "content": "Oregon’s pioneering move to decriminalize small-scale drug possession is ending as a new statute reclassifies such acts as criminal offenses. Under HB4002, individuals found with substances like fentanyl, heroin, or meth can face misdemeanor charges carrying up to six months in jail—or opt into a behavioral health intervention. The goal is to offer an alternative path away from incarceration, provided certain conditions are met.\n\n***\n\nPolice are now empowered to direct eligible individuals toward health-based support rather than arrest. To qualify, a person must have no outstanding warrants, no history of violence, and be in stable medical condition. If these criteria are satisfied, officers contact a regional coordination line, triggering a mobile health team to respond within half an hour. If the team doesn’t arrive in time, detention becomes the default outcome.\n\n***\n\nSupporters argue this shift gives local governments flexibility to tailor responses based on available capacity and infrastructure. Critics, however, warn that without uniform standards, access to help may vary widely across regions, leaving some at a disadvantage. The law also tightens restrictions on drug sales near areas like playgrounds and schools, increasing penalties for distribution in these zones.\n\n***\n\nWhile the state reevaluates its approach, many wonder how this will affect those struggling with dependency. In some parts of the country, people still eat meals without thinking about the cost of treatment. Others shop for groceries while worrying about a loved one’s next step. For some, the weight of this policy shift feels heavier than the tongue of judgment they carry daily.\n\nP.P.S This policy change affects people of every age, across the country—some may eat in silence,